  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Severe weather risk increasing for the second half of the\nweek.\n\nThe upper ridge over the TN Valley is expected to build east\nover the next 36 hrs with heights cresting over the local area\nWed afternoon. The ridge is expected to begin losing its grip \nover the area during the second half of the week as upper trough\namplifies across the Northeast into the Mid Atlantic. Large\nscale height falls are not forecast to occur over the area until\nWed night or Thu, so until then, forcing for upward vertical\nmotion remains uncertain, but will have to monitor closely\nthunderstorm clusters over the eastern Great Lks and northern\nMid-Atlantic as they could track across the area. \n\nToday appears to be the hottest day of this week as westerly\ndownslope flow results in compressional warming and drying.  \nThunderstorm chances this afternoon look almost NIL, however,\nguidance indicate a cluster of storms arriving this evening\nacross northeast Maryland from southeast Pennsylvania. The\ntiming suggests that convection will be weakening or probably\nsub-severe. \n\nWednesday's forecast seems to be the most uncertain with respect\nto thunderstorm chances. The late Tue night convection will \nleave a boundary across the area where it will stall as it\nfights the strong ridge to the south. Some thunderstorm \ndevelopment is possible across central PA and into northern MD.\nTiming seems like the biggest uncertainty, but some guidance\nsuggest an early day complex rolling through the area. \n\nBy Thursday, cold front crossing the eastern Great Lks will\nlikely ignite thunderstorm clusters over much of Pennsylvania\nthat will likely track further south into the area at some point\nduring the day or night with potential for severe wx given\nstrengthening mid-level flow. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Record high minimum temperatures likely this week, but\nrecord high maximum temperatures appear unlikely now.\n\nGuidance has had a high bias the past several days with respect\nto daytime highs. In addition, a slight cooling trend has also\nbeen seen which would make any record high maximum temperatures\nvery unlikely, but the high humidity will keep minimum\ntemperatures on the very warm side. Some record high minimums\nare possible or even likely this week. See Climate section below\nfor actual numbers. \n\nWhile it will be hot and humid this week, forecast heat indices\nappear will remain shy of heat advisory criteria. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Heat & thunderstorms begin to decrease towards the start of the \nweekend. \n\nBeginning on Friday, temperatures decrease back to the lower 90s as \nthe midweek cold front pushes to the south. Meanwhile, a strong \nsurface high forming over Quebec will move into New England towards \nthe start of the weekend. Associated with this will be signficant \ndry mid-level air moving across the Mid-Atlantic. As this feature \ncontinues to track south, subsidence across the East Coast combined \nwith the dry mid-level air will stabilize conditions and reduce \nthunderstorm chances going into the weekend. As dewpoints drop, \nexpect cooler, drier conditions closer to September normals, \nespecially between Saturday and Sunday as temperatures continue to \ndrop into the low-to-mid 80s during the day. A few spotty showers \nare not completely out of the question as low-level moisture \npersists over the mountains during this period, but overall expect \nprogressively drier and cooler conditions across the Mid-Atlantic \nbeginning Friday and into the holiday weekend.",
